---
import DraftNotice from "../components/DraftNotice.astro";
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { DOCS_URL, REPO_URL } from "../data/site.ts";
---

<Base
  title="Privacy policy"
  description="What Tessera and this website collect: nothing, today. Draft."
>
  <article class="prose">
    <h1>Privacy policy</h1>
    <DraftNotice updated="2026-10-22" />

    <h2>The short version</h2>
    <p>
      Tessera does not collect data about you. There is no telemetry, analytics, crash reporting,
      advertising or tracking in the app or on this website, and there are no plans to add any.
    </p>

    <h2>This website</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        The site is static HTML and CSS. It sets no cookies and loads nothing from other servers: no
        analytics, fonts, scripts or embeds.
      </li>
      <li>
        If you change the theme, your choice is saved in your browser's local storage so the next
        page uses it. It stays in your browser and is never sent to us. Clearing site data removes
        it.
      </li>
      <li>
        The server that hosts this site may keep standard access logs (IP address, time, page
        requested and browser user agent) to keep the site running and secure.
        <strong>To confirm before launch:</strong> who hosts the site and how long those logs are
        kept.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>The Tessera app</h2>
    <p>
      There is no release of the app yet. As it is designed, your notes are files on your own
      device, and the app sends nothing about you or your notes anywhere. You do not need an account
      to use it on one device.
    </p>

    <h2>Sync (planned)</h2>
    <p>
      Sync is not built. When it is, it will be end-to-end encrypted: notes are encrypted on your
      device before they leave it, and the sync server stores only ciphertext. A server still sees
      some metadata, such as when your devices connect, how much data you store, and which accounts
      share a vault. The <a href={`${DOCS_URL}/threat-model.md`}>threat model</a> lists exactly
      what. If you host your own server, you are the one who holds that metadata. If Vainnor ever
      offers hosted sync, this policy will be updated before anyone can sign up.
    </p>

    <h2>GitHub</h2>
    <p>
      The source code, issues and releases are on <a href={REPO_URL}>GitHub</a>. If you open an
      issue or download a release there, GitHub's own privacy statement applies to that.
    </p>

    <h2>Changes and contact</h2>
    <p>
      Changes to this page are made in public, and its history is in the repository. Questions:
      <strong>contact method to be confirmed</strong>. Until then, open a discussion on GitHub.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>
